  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 916 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nA broad look at satellite imagery this evening shows the diminishing \ninfluence of the mid/upper trough to the east with northerly flow \nset up ahead of increasing riding building in from the west. A bank \nof stratus has set up with a colder and drier airmass sinking down \nwith it, making for a chilly evening despite winds that are expected \nto trend towards calm. Overnight low forecasts continue to feel the \neffects of the low clouds, with a slight bump up to those \ntemperatures that only grace 32 degrees in northeast Nebraska, with \nthe entire area staying insulate as cold air advection at the \nsurface slows. \n\nThat stratus may be hard to kick for at least the first half of \ntomorrow, with highs once again being stymied at values within a \ndegree or two of 50. Winds will stay much quieter compared to the \nprevious day, with dissipating clouds and increasing surface \npressure set to make fog a strong possibility across the \nforecast area heading into the evening hours. Lows are also \nexpected to reach close to freezing, but not fully sink past it.\nNevertheless, those cool temperatures and what are anticipated \nto be weak winds will combine to make frost and a few areas of \nfreezing possible heading into the weekend. \n\nSaturday and Beyond:\n\nAfter that chilly morning, our mid/upper pattern begins to slowly \namplify broad ridging that centers itself over the central CONUS \nthrough Monday. High temperatures respond Saturday with a push into \nthe 50s/60s, while 60s become widespread Sunday. These temperatures \nend up being 5-10 degrees above normal for this time of year, with \nno strong winds to spoil the comfy fall temperatures. Broader model \nguidance remains in favor of a dry week as would-be rain-makers \ncontinue to miss us to the north. The west and east coasts remain \nquite active, with our next chance any any rainfall arriving \nwhenever the building trough of the Pacific Coast next week \nejects eastward. Highs during this period will continue the \nabove-normal trend, so make the most of it and enjoy some \nfestive fall outdoor time (or yard work as the leaves continue \nto pile up).",
